    32	
    33	## Statement
    34	
    35	**False claim:** every bounded sequence of reals converges
    36	([[def-sequence]], [[def-real-limit]]).
    37	
    38	The implication in the opposite direction is true and is
    39	[[lem-convergent-implies-bounded]]: every convergent sequence is bounded. The
    40	claim above asserts the converse. It is refuted by the alternating sequence
    41	$(s_k)$ and the index map defined by $n_0 = 0$ and
    42	$n_{\sigma(j)} = \sigma(\sigma(n_j))$: the refutation proves that $n$ is
    43	strictly increasing, $s_{n_j} = 1$ for every $j$, and $(s_k)$ does not
    44	converge.
    45	
    46	The sequence usually written $x_k = (-1)^k$ is introduced here by recursion
    47	([[thm-recursion]]), as the unique $(s_k)$ with $s_0 = 1$ and
    48	$s_{\sigma(k)} = -s_k$. That is the same sequence under a different presentation,
    49	and it is chosen because the three inductions of the refutation read straight off
    50	those two recursion equations, whereas the power notation would first have to be
    51	unwound into them.
    52	
    53	## Facts & Assumptions
    54	
    55	**Given:** By the recursion theorem ([[thm-recursion]]) applied to the set $\mathbb{R}$, the element $1$ and the function $u \mapsto -u$, there is a unique sequence $(s_k)$ of reals with $s_0 = 1$ and $s_{\sigma(k)} = -s_k$ for every $k$. Applied to the set $\mathbb{N}$, the element $0$ and the function $i \mapsto \sigma(\sigma(i))$, it gives a unique $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ with $n_0 = 0$ and $n_{\sigma(j)} = \sigma(\sigma(n_j))$; applied to $\mathbb{N}$, the element $\sigma(0) = 1$ and the same function, it gives a unique $m : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ with $m_0 = \sigma(0)$ and $m_{\sigma(j)} = \sigma(\sigma(m_j))$ ([[def-natural-numbers]], [[def-sequence]]).
    56	
    57	[L1] Recursion theorem ([[thm-recursion]]) and the induction principle ([[thm-induction-principle]]).
    58	
    59	[L2] Absolute value and field arithmetic: $|-u| = |u|$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]); $|1| = 1$, because $1 > 0$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]]) and $|v| = v$ whenever $v \ge 0$ by the definition of the absolute value ([[def-real-order]], [[def-abs-value]]); and $-(-u) = u$ ([[def-field]]).
    60	
    61	[L3] Order in $\mathbb{R}$: $0 < 1$, sums of positives are positive, and adding a constant preserves the order, so $1 - (-1) = 1 + 1 > 0$ and hence $-1 < 1$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]], [[lem-of-add-order]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]).
    62	
    63	[L4] Order on $\mathbb{N}$: $i < \sigma(i)$ for every $i$, because $\sigma(i) = i + 1$ and $\sigma(i) \ne i$; and the order is transitive ([[def-nat-order]], [[def-nat-addition]], [[lem-nat-successor-neq-self]], [[thm-nat-linear-order]]).
    64	
    65	[L5] Consecutive comparisons suffice for strict increase ([[lem-index-map-grows]]).
    66	
    67	[L6] Convergence, and the fact that a constant sequence converges to its value ([[def-real-limit]], [[def-sequence]]).
    68	
    69	[L7] Divergence test: a sequence with two subsequences converging to different limits does not converge ([[lem-subsequence-inherits-limit]]).
    70	
    71	[L8] Boundedness of a sequence ([[def-sequence]]).
    72	
    73	## Refutation
    74	
    75	**Proof technique:** direct.
    76	
    77	1.1 By induction, $|s_k| = 1$ for every $k$: the base case is $|s_0| = |1| = 1$, and if $|s_k| = 1$ then $|s_{\sigma(k)}| = |-s_k| = |s_k| = 1$. Hence $|s_k| \le 1$ for every $k$, so $(s_k)$ is bounded. [L1, L2, L8]
    78	
    79	1.2 By induction, $s_{n_j} = 1$ for every $j$: the base case is $s_{n_0} = s_0 = 1$, and if $s_{n_j} = 1$ then $s_{n_{\sigma(j)}} = s_{\sigma(\sigma(n_j))} = -s_{\sigma(n_j)} = -(-s_{n_j}) = s_{n_j} = 1$. [L1, L2]
    80	
    81	1.3 By induction, $s_{m_j} = -1$ for every $j$: the base case is $s_{m_0} = s_{\sigma(0)} = -s_0 = -1$, and if $s_{m_j} = -1$ then $s_{m_{\sigma(j)}} = s_{\sigma(\sigma(m_j))} = -(-s_{m_j}) = s_{m_j} = -1$. [L1, L2]
    82	
    83	1.4 Both $n$ and $m$ are strictly increasing: for every $j$ we have $n_j < \sigma(n_j) < \sigma(\sigma(n_j)) = n_{\sigma(j)}$ by [L4], and likewise $m_j < m_{\sigma(j)}$, so [L5] applies to each. [L1, L4, L5]
    84	
    85	1.5 The reals $1$ and $-1$ are distinct, since $1 - (-1) = 1 + 1 > 0$. [L3]
    86	
    87	2.1 By step 1.4 the maps $n$ and $m$ define subsequences $(s_{n_j})_j$ and $(s_{m_j})_j$ of $(s_k)$; by steps 1.2 and 1.3 these are the constant sequences $1$ and $-1$, so they converge to $1$ and to $-1$ respectively, and by step 1.5 those two limits differ. [step 1.2, step 1.3, step 1.4, step 1.5, L6, L8]
    88	
    89	3.1 By the divergence test, $(s_k)$ does not converge. [step 2.1, L7]
    90	
    91	4.1 So $(s_k)$ is a bounded sequence of reals that does not converge, by steps 1.1 and 3.1; the claim that every bounded sequence converges is therefore false. [step 1.1, step 3.1] ∎
    92	
    93	## Remarks
    94	
    95	- The refutation is self-contained: the witness is constructed by recursion, its boundedness and its two subsequential limits are each proved by induction, and the failure of convergence comes from the divergence test of [[lem-subsequence-inherits-limit]] together with uniqueness of limits ([[lem-limit-unique]]).
    96	
    97	- **What is true in this direction** is the Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem: every bounded sequence of reals has a convergent *subsequence*. That is a genuine theorem and it needs the least-upper-bound property. It is **not proved anywhere in this library as things stand**: it is the subject of the next page of this track, *Monotone Sequences, Bolzano-Weierstrass, and Cauchy Completeness*, which is planned and not yet written. It is named here only to say what the correct statement is; nothing above uses it, and no item of this library may be cited for it. The false claim above is what one gets by deleting the word "subsequence" from it.
    98	
    99	- The error is tempting because boundedness feels like "no room to escape". It is not: boundedness forbids running away, but it does not forbid oscillating forever, and oscillation is exactly what $(s_k)$ does.
   100	
   101	- The same witness refutes [[fs-subsequence-convergence-implies-convergence]], and it is the sequence referred to in the remarks of [[lem-limit-abs]].

