    31	---
    32	
    33	## Statement
    34	
    35	Every bounded sequence of reals has a convergent subsequence: if $(x_k)$ is a
    36	sequence of reals and there is $M \in \mathbb{R}$ with $|x_k| \le M$ for every
    37	$k \in \mathbb{N}$ ([[def-sequence]]), then there is a strictly increasing
    38	$n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ and a real $L$ with $x_{n_j} \to L$.
    39	
    40	Equivalently: the subsequential limit set of a bounded sequence is nonempty
    41	([[def-subsequential-limit]]).
    42	
    43	The theorem is the exact repair of the false claim that a bounded sequence
    44	converges. A bounded
    45	sequence need not converge, and the alternating sequence is the standing witness;
    46	what boundedness does force is that *some* subsequence converges. The converse of
    47	the theorem is false, and badly so: a sequence with a convergent subsequence need
    48	not be bounded.
    49	
    50	## Facts & Assumptions
    51	
    52	**Given:** A sequence $(x_k)$ of reals and a real $M$ with $|x_k| \le M$ for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$.
    53	
    54	[L1] Every sequence of reals has a monotone subsequence ([[lem-peak-monotone-subsequence]]).
    55	
    56	[L2] A monotone sequence of reals converges if and only if it is bounded ([[cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded]]).
    57	
    58	[L3] A subsequence $(x_{n_j})$ of $(x_k)$ along a strictly increasing $n$ is again a sequence of reals, and each of its terms is a term of $(x_k)$; a sequence is bounded when some $M$ satisfies $|{\cdot}| \le M$ at every index ([[def-sequence]]).
    59	
    60	[L4] Monotone means nondecreasing or nonincreasing ([[def-monotone-sequence]]).
    61	
    62	[L5] $L$ is a subsequential limit of $(x_k)$ when some subsequence of $(x_k)$ converges to $L$ ([[def-subsequential-limit]]).
    63	
    64	## Proof
    65	
    66	**Proof technique:** direct.
    67	
    68	1.1 By [L1] fix a strictly increasing $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ such that the subsequence $(x_{n_j})$ is monotone; no hypothesis on $(x_k)$ is needed for this step. [given, L1, L4, choose]
    69	
    70	2.1 $(x_{n_j})$ is bounded: each of its terms is a term of $(x_k)$, so $|x_{n_j}| \le M$ for every $j$, with the same $M$. [step 1.1, given, L3]
    71	
    72	3.1 Being monotone and bounded, $(x_{n_j})$ converges; write $L$ for its limit. [step 1.1, step 2.1, L2]
    73	
    74	4.1 So $(x_k)$ has a convergent subsequence, and $L$ is a subsequential limit of $(x_k)$; in particular the subsequential limit set of a bounded sequence is nonempty. [step 3.1, L5] ∎
    75	
    76	## Remarks
    77	
    78	- **The proof is two citations, and that is the point of the page order.** All
    79	  the work sits in [[lem-peak-monotone-subsequence]], which needs nothing about
    80	  $\mathbb{R}$ beyond trichotomy, and in [[cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded]],
    81	  which is where the least-upper-bound property is actually spent. Splitting the
    82	  argument this way isolates the use of completeness in a single place instead of
    83	  burying it in a bisection.
    84	
    85	- **Bisection is the other standard proof and is not used here.** Halving the
    86	  interval $[-M, M]$ repeatedly and keeping a half containing infinitely many
    87	  terms produces a nested sequence of intervals whose lengths tend to $0$, and
    88	  [[thm-nested-interval-property]] then yields the limit. That route is
    89	  available in this library, since the nested interval property is proved on this
    90	  page, but it needs an extra argument to choose the terms and to see that the
    91	  chosen indices increase, whereas the monotone-subsequence route needs neither.
    92	
    93	- **The limit is not determined by the theorem.** A bounded sequence may have
    94	  many subsequential limits, and the theorem asserts only that there is at least
    95	  one. Which subsequential limits exist, and that there is a largest and a
    96	  smallest, is the subject of the $\limsup$ page.
    97	
    98	- **Boundedness is sufficient but not necessary.** The converse fails, by
    99	  [[fs-convergent-subsequence-implies-bounded]] and its witness
   100	  [[cex-unbounded-with-convergent-subsequence]]: a wildly unbounded sequence can
   101	  still have a constant, hence convergent, subsequence.
