    30	---
    31	
    32	## Statement
    33	
    34	Let $(x_k)$ be a Cauchy sequence of reals ([[def-real-limit]]) and suppose some
    35	subsequence $(x_{n_j})$ converges to $L \in \mathbb{R}$, that is, $L$ is a
    36	subsequential limit of $(x_k)$ ([[def-subsequential-limit]]). Then the whole
    37	sequence $(x_k)$ converges, and its limit is $L$.
    38	
    39	So for a Cauchy sequence a single convergent subsequence already determines the
    40	behaviour of the sequence. This is exactly the step that upgrades
    41	Bolzano-Weierstrass into Cauchy completeness in
    42	the Cauchy criterion later on this page, and it is false without the Cauchy
    43	hypothesis.
    44	
    45	## Facts & Assumptions
    46	
    47	**Given:** A Cauchy sequence $(x_k)$ of reals, a strictly increasing $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$, and $L \in \mathbb{R}$ with $x_{n_j} \to L$.
    48	
    49	[A1] Cauchy condition: for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K$ with $|x_k - x_l| < \varepsilon$ for all $k, l \ge K$ ([[def-real-limit]]).
    50	
    51	[A2] Convergence of the subsequence: for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $J$ with $|x_{n_j} - L| < \varepsilon$ for all $j \ge J$ ([[def-real-limit]], [[def-subsequential-limit]], [[def-sequence]]).
    52	
    53	[L1] Triangle inequality: $|x - L| = |(x - y) + (y - L)| \le |x - y| + |y - L|$ ([[lem-of-triangle-inequality]]).
    54	
    55	[L2] Growth of an index map: a strictly increasing $n$ satisfies $n_j \ge j$ for every $j$ ([[lem-index-map-grows]]).
    56	
    57	[L3] Halving a rational: if $\varepsilon$ is a positive rational then so is $\varepsilon/2$, and the embedding of $\mathbb{Q}$ in $\mathbb{R}$ is a field embedding, so the image of $\varepsilon/2$ is half the image of $\varepsilon$ and the two halves sum to $\varepsilon$ ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]).
    58	
    59	[L4] The order on $\mathbb{N}$ is total and transitive, so two indices $J, K$ admit an index $j$ with $j \ge J$ and $j \ge K$ ([[thm-nat-linear-order]]).
    60	
    61	[L5] Convergence: $(x_k)$ converges to $L$ when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K$ with $|x_k - L| < \varepsilon$ for all $k \ge K$ ([[def-real-limit]]).
    62	
    63	## Proof
    64	
    65	**Proof technique:** direct.
    66	
    67	1.1 Let $\varepsilon > 0$ be an arbitrary rational; then $\varepsilon/2$ is again a positive rational, and $\varepsilon/2 + \varepsilon/2 = \varepsilon$. [given, L3]
    68	
    69	2.1 By [A1] applied to $\varepsilon/2$, fix $K \in \mathbb{N}$ with $|x_k - x_l| < \varepsilon/2$ for all $k, l \ge K$. [step 1.1, A1, choose]
    70	
    71	2.2 By [A2] applied to $\varepsilon/2$, fix $J \in \mathbb{N}$ with $|x_{n_j} - L| < \varepsilon/2$ for all $j \ge J$. [step 1.1, A2, choose]
    72	
    73	3.1 Fix a single index $j$ with $j \ge J$ and $j \ge K$; then $n_j \ge j \ge K$, so the term $x_{n_j}$ is simultaneously within $\varepsilon/2$ of $L$ and within $\varepsilon/2$ of every $x_k$ with $k \ge K$. [step 2.1, step 2.2, L2, L4, choose]
    74	
    75	4.1 For every $k \ge K$: $|x_k - L| \le |x_k - x_{n_j}| + |x_{n_j} - L| < \varepsilon/2 + \varepsilon/2 = \varepsilon$. [step 2.1, step 2.2, step 3.1, L1]
    76	
    77	5.1 The rational $\varepsilon > 0$ was arbitrary and an index $K$ was produced for it, so $(x_k)$ converges to $L$. [step 4.1, L5] ∎
    78	
    79	## Remarks
    80	
    81	- **The Cauchy hypothesis is doing all the work.** Without it a convergent
    82	  subsequence says nothing at all about the sequence, which is
    83	  [[fs-subsequence-convergence-implies-convergence]]; the alternating sequence
    84	  has a constant, hence convergent, subsequence and does not converge. What the
    85	  Cauchy condition adds is that the terms are eventually close to *each other*,
    86	  so being close to $L$ at one late index propagates to all late indices.
    87	
    88	- **The single index $j$ chosen in step 3.1 is the whole trick.** It is used
    89	  once, as a bridge, and is not required to grow with $k$; this is why $n_j \ge j$
    90	  ([[lem-index-map-grows]]) is needed only to know that some subsequence index
    91	  lies beyond $K$.
    92	
    93	- **The limit is forced to be $L$, not merely to exist.** Combined with
    94	  uniqueness of limits ([[lem-limit-unique]]), this says that a Cauchy sequence
    95	  has at most one subsequential limit, so for Cauchy sequences the subsequential
    96	  limit set ([[def-subsequential-limit]]) is empty or a single point, and
    97	  [[thm-cauchy-criterion-via-lub]] rules out the empty case in $\mathbb{R}$.
