items/lem-index-map-grows.md:62:[A1] $Q(k)$ denotes the statement: $n_j < n_k$ for every $j < k$.
items/lem-index-map-grows.md:64:[A2] $P(k)$ denotes the statement: $n_k \ge k$.
items/lem-index-map-grows.md:66:[L1] Order and successor on $\mathbb{N}$: $m \le p$ means $m + i = p$ for some $i$, so $0 \le m$ for every $m$ because $0 + m = m$; and $\sigma(k) = k + 1$ with $\sigma(k) \ne k$, so $k < \sigma(k)$ ([[def-nat-order]], [[def-nat-addition]], [[lem-nat-add-identity]], [[lem-nat-successor-neq-self]]).
items/lem-index-map-grows.md:68:[L2] Discreteness: $m < p$ if and only if $\sigma(m) \le p$ ([[lem-nat-discrete]]).
items/lem-index-map-grows.md:70:[L3] Induction principle: if $R(0)$ holds and $R(k)$ implies $R(\sigma(k))$ for every $k$, then $R(k)$ holds for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$ ([[thm-induction-principle]]).
items/lem-index-map-grows.md:72:[L4] The order on $\mathbb{N}$ is reflexive, antisymmetric, transitive and total, and satisfies trichotomy ([[thm-nat-linear-order]], [[lem-nat-trichotomy]]).
items/fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality.md:47:[L1] Convergence, quantified over rational $\varepsilon > 0$; a constant sequence converges to its value ([[def-real-limit]], [[def-sequence]]).
items/fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality.md:49:[L2] Archimedean property: for every $z \in \mathbb{R}$ there is a natural $N \ge 1$ with $z < N \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}}$ ([[thm-of-archimedean]]).
items/fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality.md:51:[L3] Canonical naturals: $n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}} > 0$ for every $n \ge 1$, and $n \mapsto n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}}$ is strictly increasing on $\{1, 2, 3, \dots\}$ ([[lem-of-naturals-positive]]).
items/fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality.md:53:[L4] Inverses and order: $a > 0$ implies $a^{-1} > 0$; $0 < a < b$ implies $0 < b^{-1} < a^{-1}$; and $(u^{-1})^{-1} = u$ for $u \ne 0$ ([[lem-of-inverse-positive]], [[def-field]]).
items/fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality.md:55:[L5] Absolute value: $|u| = u$ when $u \ge 0$, and $|u - 0| = |u|$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]], [[def-real-order]]).
items/fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality.md:57:[L6] Order arithmetic: transitivity and trichotomy in $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]). On $\mathbb{N}$, $m < n$ if and only if $\sigma(m) \le n$, so $\sigma(k) = k + 1$ is the immediate successor of $k$ ([[lem-nat-discrete]]); transitivity of the linear order therefore gives $k \ge N \Rightarrow k + 1 > N$ ([[thm-nat-linear-order]]).
items/fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality.md:59:[L7] Limits preserve non-strict inequalities ([[lem-limit-preserves-order]]).
items/fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality.md:61:[L8] A sequence of reals has at most one limit ([[lem-limit-unique]]), so the symbols $\lim_k x_k$ and $\lim_k y_k$ appearing in the false claim and below denote.
items/fs-subsequence-convergence-implies-convergence.md:57:[L1] Established in [[fs-bounded-implies-convergent]]: the map $n$ is strictly increasing; $s_{n_j} = 1$ for every $j$; and $(s_k)$ does not converge.
items/fs-subsequence-convergence-implies-convergence.md:59:[L2] A constant sequence converges to its value ([[def-real-limit]], [[def-sequence]]).
items/fs-subsequence-convergence-implies-convergence.md:61:[L3] Subsequences are the composites along strictly increasing index maps ([[def-sequence]]).
items/fs-subsequence-convergence-implies-convergence.md:63:[L4] Every subsequence of a convergent sequence converges to the same limit ([[lem-subsequence-inherits-limit]]), and a sequence is a subsequence of itself along the identity index map, which is strictly increasing ([[def-sequence]]).
items/fs-bounded-implies-convergent.md:57:[L1] Recursion theorem ([[thm-recursion]]) and the induction principle ([[thm-induction-principle]]).
items/fs-bounded-implies-convergent.md:59:[L2] Absolute value and field arithmetic: $|-u| = |u|$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]); $|1| = 1$, because $1 > 0$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]]) and $|v| = v$ whenever $v \ge 0$ by the definition of the absolute value ([[def-real-order]], [[def-abs-value]]); and $-(-u) = u$ ([[def-field]]).
items/fs-bounded-implies-convergent.md:61:[L3] Order in $\mathbb{R}$: $0 < 1$, sums of positives are positive, and adding a constant preserves the order, so $1 - (-1) = 1 + 1 > 0$ and hence $-1 < 1$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]], [[lem-of-add-order]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]).
